{
 "_provenance": {
  "what": "Every number the Sūrya-Siddhānta states in word-numerals (bhūtasaṅkhyā) in its numeric verses, decoded from the verse's own words: first word = units; a word worth more than 9 contributes its digits reversed (tithi 15 → 5,1).",
  "why": "The editions and their renderings have printed several of these wrongly and then called the slips 'variants'. The verse words are the source; the test re-decodes them, checks the text's own internal proofs (the versed-sine table is R minus the sine table read backwards; the yuga counts follow from three integers), and checks that the books print these numbers and not the slips.",
  "edition": "editions/surya-siddhanta-full-edition.html",
  "forbidden": "per verse, numbers the edition once printed that the verse words contradict; the test fails if any reappears in that verse's block (decimals such as 224.85 are allowed)",
  "plain": "Numbers stated in ordinary number words (not bhūtasaṅkhyā). Checked by utsava.test.js: 14.4-14.6 interlock (Tulā 0° + k×86° gives the four degrees of 14.5, and 4×86 + 16 = 360), and 4.1-4.3 give the disc used by 14.11.",
  "edition_forbidden": "Slips once printed in the edition and corrected on 2026-10-07. ss-numbers.test.js strips the correction notes and fails if any of these appears anywhere else in the book."
 },
